==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|fimf|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|pénkʷe||five|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognates include Old High German {{term|fimf|lang=goh}}, ''finf'' (German ''[[fünf]]''), Old Saxon {{term|fif|fīf|lang=osx}}, Old Dutch ''fīf'', {{term|vif|vīf|lang=odt}} (Dutch [[vijf]]), Old Norse ''[[fimm]]'' (Swedish ''[[fem]]''), Gothic {{term|𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek {{term|πέντε|tr=pente|lang=el}}, Latin ''[[quinque]]'', Sanskrit {{term|पञ्चन्|lang=sa}}.

